a coordination wait must be bounded

With launchd running, launchd registers com.apple.FileCoordination as a MachService NAME and never
starts the job, so the send succeeds, the port is valid, and no reply ever arrives. The synchronous
path then sat in dispatch_semaphore_wait with DISPATCH_TIME_FOREVER on the very FIRST intent, for
the whole run.

Measured, with the daemon proved absent rather than assumed: /var/log/fcdaemon.log, which the job
plist asks for by name with FCD_DEBUG set, is never created, and no filecoordinationd process exists
when the guest is inspected from the host by /proc/pid/exe.

Coordination is advisory, so giving up and proceeding is the lesser failure. The first caller pays
the whole timeout and latches, so later callers pay a token wait instead of the full five seconds
each.

The semaphore needed an owner for this to be safe: a bounded wait can return before the reply
arrives, so the reply block now holds its own reference and releases it, rather than signalling a
semaphore the waiting frame has already freed.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, stated because the capture was looked at: iA Writer under launchd still shows
an EMPTY file list. The hang is gone and the timeout fires twice as designed, and the list is empty
anyway, so that has a second cause which is not this one. Without launchd, where the daemon answers
with an error immediately, nothing changes: a fresh index still fills every body column and the
counters still read four of four.

